From: OCCTSwift ecosystem <noreply@secondmouse.au>
Subject: [PATCH] Mesh - BRepMesh_IncrementalMesh::initParameters refuses a NaN
 parameter instead of handing it to the mesher

BRepMesh_IncrementalMesh::initParameters validates five meshing parameters, and
every one of the five tests is written as `value < bound`:

  if (myParameters.Deflection < Precision::Confusion())         { throw ... }
  if (myParameters.DeflectionInterior < Precision::Confusion()) { ... = Deflection; }
  if (myParameters.MinSize < Precision::Confusion())            { ... = recomputed; }
  if (myParameters.Angle < Precision::Angular())                { throw ... }
  if (myParameters.AngleInterior < Precision::Angular())        { ... = 2.0 * Angle; }

Every comparison with NaN is false, so a NaN parameter satisfies none of the
five: the two tests that refuse do not refuse, the three that substitute a
usable value do not substitute, and the NaN reaches the mesher intact. The
function exists so that no value outside its bounds gets that far, and NaN is
outside every one of them.

Spelling each test !(value >= bound) puts NaN on the refusing or substituting
branch, because an unordered comparison makes >= false and the negation true.
For every ordered value the two spellings are the same test, so no bound moves
and no valid input changes behaviour. This is a NaN fix only.

Ordering then holds where it has to. Deflection and Angle are validated before
the expressions that consume them, so `(std::min)(Deflection, DeflectionInterior)`
inside the MinSize substitution and `2.0 * myParameters.Angle` in the
AngleInterior substitution can no longer produce a NaN of their own from a NaN
they were handed.

Measured on macOS arm64 against the pinned V8_0_1 asset, one process per case
with an external timeout, because a tessellation that does not return is not
catchable in-process.

Linear, Scripts/repro/2879/, on BRepPrimAPI_MakeCylinder(10, 5):

  deflection       result
  0.1              130 nodes, 1 s
  1e-4             3,978 nodes, 1 s
  1e-5             12,570 nodes, 5 s
  1e-6             39,742 nodes, 72 s
  1e-7, the floor  88,862 nodes, 92 s
  9e-8, 1e-12,     Standard_NumericError from initParameters, under 1 s each
  0.0, -1.0
  NaN              did not return in 600 s

On a box, whose faces are all planar, the same NaN returned a mesh at no stated
deflection (24 nodes); on a free circular edge it gave 22,216 Poly_Polygon3D
nodes where a valid request gives 33. One input, three different wrong answers,
none of them a refusal. The ordinary too-small value is already refused
promptly, so NaN is the single input that walks through, and 1e-7 terminating
in 92 s is why the floor itself is not the problem.

Angular, Scripts/repro/2900/, same cylinder, at linear deflection 10.0 so that
the angle is the criterion that decides:

  angle   result
  0.05    254 nodes
  0.2     254 nodes
  0.5     106 nodes
  1.0     54 nodes
  9e-13   Standard_NumericError from initParameters
  0.0     Standard_NumericError from initParameters
  -1.0    Standard_NumericError from initParameters
  NaN     IsDone() true, Angle NaN, AngleInterior NaN, 18 nodes

So the angular hole has a different symptom from the linear one and is worse to
diagnose: no hang and no refusal, just the coarsest mesh the linear rule alone
will accept, reported as done. The two travel as one change because they are one
mechanism in one function, reached by one set of callers.

The three substituting tests are in the change for the function's own invariant
rather than for a measured defect, and the difference is worth stating. A NaN
AngleInterior reaching initParameters with a valid Angle was measured in the
same run and changes neither the node count nor the triangle count on either
fixture: 106 nodes on the cylinder, the same as a valid interior angle gives.
DeflectionInterior and MinSize were not separately measured. All three are
included because a function whose contract is that nothing out of bounds reaches
the mesher cannot leave three of its five tests permeable to the one value that
defeats all five, and because the substitutions the two throwing guards protect
are only finite once those guards are in place.

initParameters is the one place the kernel checks these values, so it is the
one place the fix is needed. Prs3d::GetDeflection (Prs3d.hxx:71) and the incmesh
DRAW command (MeshTest.cxx:199, :208) share the blind spot in their own
std::max and <= tests, but every value they produce arrives here.

The check is a literal throw in an inline header function rather than a
*_Raise_if, so Standard_NoException does not remove it and the refusal is live
in a release build.

SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift#2879, SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift#2900.
---
 src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKMesh/BRepMesh/BRepMesh_IncrementalMesh.hxx | 11 ++++++-----
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKMesh/BRepMesh/BRepMesh_IncrementalMesh.hxx b/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKMesh/BRepMesh/BRepMesh_IncrementalMesh.hxx
--- a/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKMesh/BRepMesh/BRepMesh_IncrementalMesh.hxx
+++ b/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKMesh/BRepMesh/BRepMesh_IncrementalMesh.hxx
@@ -78,17 +78,18 @@
   //! Initializes specific parameters
   void initParameters()
   {
-    if (myParameters.Deflection < Precision::Confusion())
+    // Each test is spelled !(value >= bound) so that a NaN parameter takes the refusing branch.
+    if (!(myParameters.Deflection >= Precision::Confusion()))
     {
       throw Standard_NumericError(
         "BRepMesh_IncrementalMesh::initParameters : invalid parameter value");
     }
-    if (myParameters.DeflectionInterior < Precision::Confusion())
+    if (!(myParameters.DeflectionInterior >= Precision::Confusion()))
     {
       myParameters.DeflectionInterior = myParameters.Deflection;
     }
 
-    if (myParameters.MinSize < Precision::Confusion())
+    if (!(myParameters.MinSize >= Precision::Confusion()))
     {
       myParameters.MinSize =
         (std::max)(IMeshTools_Parameters::RelMinSize()
@@ -96,12 +97,12 @@
                    Precision::Confusion());
     }
 
-    if (myParameters.Angle < Precision::Angular())
+    if (!(myParameters.Angle >= Precision::Angular()))
     {
       throw Standard_NumericError(
         "BRepMesh_IncrementalMesh::initParameters : invalid parameter value");
     }
-    if (myParameters.AngleInterior < Precision::Angular())
+    if (!(myParameters.AngleInterior >= Precision::Angular()))
     {
       myParameters.AngleInterior = 2.0 * myParameters.Angle;
     }
